  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1232 PM MST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nWhile fog and low clouds persist through the short term period, a \nmore active weather pattern begins the long term period. A robust \ntrough will push into the western coast of the US and progress \nfurther towards NM. There is good model agreement that a shortwave \nprogresses through the trough and reaches the Four Corners region by \nearly to midday Tuesday. As it does, models also agree on the trough \nbecoming more negatively tilted, adjusting the flow of the trough to \nbe southwesterly. 700mb temperatures also look to be 1C to 3C before \nthe trough passage, dropping to -1C to -3C after the passage. With \nall this combined, rain and high elevation snow showers look poised \nto develop Tuesday afternoon and continue through Wednesday morning. \nOverall rainfall amounts look low at this time, with only a few \nhundredths to 0.1-0.2\" across the area. The higher amounts for this \nsystem, and where snowfall is likely to occur, will be in the \nnorthern mountains, particularly the Tusas Mountains. Snow levels \nare forecast to be ~9,000-9,500ft, with locations above that in the \nTusas Mountains forecast roughly 2-4\" at this time. Also cannot rule \nout a few inches across parts of the Sangre de Cristo Mountains at a \nsimilar snow level. Below the rain/snow line, a few areas may see up \nto 0.25-0.5\" of rainfall, primarily driven by orographic forcing. A \nquestion with this system is how much moisture will actually be \npresent within it. The ECMWF is a bit wetter than the GFS at this \ntime, and progresses the colder 700mb temperatures faster into NM, \nthough it's resolution makes it difficult to resolve terrain snow. \nWe'll have to keep watch on the progression of the system for if a \nWinter Weather Advisory will be necessary. \n\nA secondary feature with the approaching trough will be stronger \nsouthwest 700mb winds. At this time, the winds look to be passing \nthrough the state during the nighttime hours of Tuesday, which \nbrings into question whether these winds can mix down to the surface \nwith nighttime inversions forming. To counteract that, a surface \npressure gradient looks to develop across much of eastern NM (and \nespecially in northeast NM). Afternoon and overnight breezy to \nlocally windy conditions are likely to develop, with those winds \ncontinuing across portions of eastern NM into Wednesday morning.\n\nThe latter half of Wednesday and Thursday look to be fairly tranquil \nin comparison as weaker upper level flow sits over the state and we \nplace ourselves in between two systems. The next system to approach \nNM looks to occur late Thursday through Friday. This comes in the \nform of another robust trough moving inland towards the Four Corners \nregion, which is forecast to move over the state and provide \nadditional precipitation and winds. Recent model runs have shown \nthat the trough axis has slowly been trending north, which would \nsuggest the bulk of the precipitation developed by this system \nmisses NM. Nevertheless, this system looks to continue a stretch of \nactive weather across the region with rain and snow showers. In \nterms of winds, 700mb winds look similar to the first system \ncrossing Tuesday, however this system is currently progged to cross \nthe state during peak heating hours, allowing for easier mixing of \nupper level winds to the surface. Thus, forecast winds are higher \nacross much of the state, especially eastern NM.",
